russia is starting joint military drills  with its neighbor belarus today nato  says it is moscow's largest deployment  to the former soviet country since the  cold war the exercises come as tensions  build over what some western countries  fear are russian plans to invade ukraine  diplomacy is continuing but the u.s has  criticized the new joint military drills  as escalatory kiev says these are about  psychological pressure russia claims the  forces will return to their bases when  the drills finish in 10 days time  the u.s is also deploying more troops to  eastern europe as a show of solidarity  they're bolstering nato's borders  on wednesday u.s troops arrived in  southeastern poland  the united states has also begun moving  its forces from germany to romania  around a thousand nato troops are  expected to follow in the coming days  denmark has also announced that it's  enhancing its military preparedness  deploying fighter jets to the baltic sea  but many western analysts do not see  nato's actions in response to the more  than 100 000 russian troops amassed near  ukraine's borders as a direct defense of  ukraine now in terms of numbers this is  meant to be a symbolic show of resolve  that the  nato is united and it will defend its  members if attacked for ukraine we've  provided arms and training but we're not  providing any direct forces nobody is  further consignments of british weapons  have arrived in ukraine  britain has said it only intends them to  be used in self-defense  speaking in berlin on wednesday german  chancellor olaf schultz said a dual  approach was needed adding that while  all diplomatic channels must be pursued  allies must be prepared to act if  russian forces cross these borders and  into ukraine  for more perspective on the story we can  speak now to carl belt he is the  co-chair of the european council of  foreign relations and former prime  minister of sweden mr belt welcome tdw i  want to start with a russia's military  drills russia says these drills are for  its own security ukraine says this is  psychological pressure do you think that  these drills are a further escalation of  the situation  they are clearly one part of the very  major military buildup that we've seen  from the russian side during the last  few months and it is still ongoing apart  from this maneuvers in belarus we have a  significant concentration of russian  forces along the borders of ukraine and  is the largest such buildup of military  forces that we've seen in in europe for  decades we have to go way back into the  cold war to say anything like this  i want to ask you now about nato's  response to all of this we saw the  secretary general ian stoltenberg  speaking at a news conference a short  while ago let's uh play first of all  what he said and then talk about it  let's listen in  this is a dangerous moment for european  security  the number of russian forces is going up  the warning time for a possible attack  is going down  nato is not a threat to russia  but we must be prepared for the worst  while remaining strongly committed to  finding a political solution  mr bilt we heard yen stoltenberg say  this is preparing for the worst from the  perspective of nato and you wrote  recently on twitter it is a good sign  that germany for example is upping its  contribution to the nato battalion and  lithuania because this is a reassurance  in uncertain times but still these nato  forces pale in comparison to what russia  has been preparing so does this really  act as a deterrent to russia  i think the main deterrent is as a  matter of fact the preparations that are  done for very significant sanctions by  the eu and and by the u.s in case  there's another russian innovation of uh  ukraine uh those sanctions have been and  that's the ongoing process coordinated  between washington and brussels and then  coordinated between brussels and the  different member states  and uh the way i understand it uh they  would be very significant and have a  very significant effect on the russian  economy and hopefully that will serve as  a deterrent so that uh there is no  further incursions and invasions of  ukraine but we don't know  but there has been some disagreement  among western allies of what those  sanctions should be  do you think that europe's reliance on  russian gas is serving as a liability at  this point  i think it's clearly a problem um and i  think there is an awareness  all over europe i think even in germany  that long term there would be sort of a  need to reduce that reliance on russian  gas because it provides russia with  leverage in political situations like  this then replacement of that can't  happen overnight  but i think over years absolutely i  think that needs to be  a redirection of  energy dependence  i want to ask you about the west putting  its hopes on the long stuck mince  agreement that was first negotiated in  2014 and 2015.  ukrainian officials say that this peace  deal itself is a weapon that moscow is  using to try to destroy their country  what is your assessment  well  if you take the russian interpretation  of it absolutely  then the russians are trying to use the  minsk agreement to that particular  effect that is not going to be accepted  by  kiev obviously  it is an agreement that isn't entirely  clear there's a rather dubious element  of it and there has been a significant  disagreement between moscow and kiev on  the timing of it on local elections and  special status and who's going to  control the borders so it's a very sort  of uh  it wasn't drafted with too much of it  just precision and that is used by  moscow very much in order to  impose upon ukraine a version of the  minsk agreement that is clearly  detrimental to the security interests of  the country that's resisted  by kiev understandably so  i want to ask you last of all mr bilt  about the fact that the german  chancellor olaf schultz has said he is  hopeful  that there can be good progress made  particularly this week on finding a  diplomatic solution to this crisis as  things stand at the moment with these  military drills taking place uh from the  russian side today the response from  ukraine are you confident that there is  still an avenue for a diplomatic  solution  well  i would agree with the chancellor that  one should be hopeful but but one should  also be really realistic i mean it's  good that sort of berlin and paris is  now sort of accelerating efforts on  donbass on the means process but we  should understand that the russian game  at the moment is much bigger uh donbass  is a sideshow i have to say the russian  game if you listen to mr putin and see  the documents that they've put on the  table is about ukraine as a whole and  it's about sort of changing the entire  european security order and that is a  negotiation that they undertaken  primarily with  the us and with nato that's the big game  that we see at the moment  we'll have to leave it there carl bill  the co-chair of the european council  foreign relations former prime minister  of sweden thank you very much for  joining us  thank you  you
 

